course: geography
lesson: 37
title: How empires fell: the map of the 20th century
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: In 1914 a world map looked like a few huge patches of colour: European empires, with the USA and Japan, ruled most of the land and people on Earth, and the British Empire alone later covered about a quarter of the world's land.
explain: Within one lifetime those patches broke into many small ones, like a giant jigsaw spilled on the floor and put back as dozens of separate pictures.
explain: After the First World War four old empires fell apart; after the Second, colony after colony in Asia and Africa became free, some by long campaigns like Gandhi's in India, some only after cruel wars like Algeria's.
explain: Europa Universalis 5 ends in 1836, when the empires are still growing, but its colonial map is already cracking: most of Spanish America and Brazil had become independent by then.
explain: The breaking up went on to the end of the century: the Soviet Union split into 15 countries in 1991, and Hong Kong and Macau went back to China in 1997 and 1999.
fact: In 1914 European empires, along with the USA and Japan, ruled most of the world's land and people.
fact: The British Empire at its largest, around 1920, covered about a quarter of the world's land.
fact: After the First World War, the Austro-Hungarian, Russian, German and Ottoman empires broke apart, and countries such as Poland, Czechoslovakia, Yugoslavia, Finland and the Baltic states appeared or reappeared.
fact: The Ottoman Empire ended in 1922, and the Republic of Turkey was founded in 1923.
fact: Borders such as those of Iraq, Syria and Jordan were largely drawn by Britain and France after the First World War.
fact: Most Spanish colonies in the Americas, and Portuguese Brazil, became independent between about 1810 and 1825, with leaders such as Simón Bolívar.
fact: Between about 1945 and 1975, as empires weakened by the Second World War faced long campaigns for freedom, most colonies in Asia and Africa became independent countries, and UN membership grew from 51 in 1945 to more than 140 by 1975.
fact: India won independence from Britain in 1947 after decades of peaceful campaigning led by figures such as Mahatma Gandhi, whose Salt March of 1930 protested against a British tax on salt.
fact: Ghana became independent in 1957, the first colony in sub-Saharan Africa to do so.
fact: In 1960 alone, 17 African countries became independent, and it is called the Year of Africa.
fact: Some colonies won independence only after long wars, such as Algeria from France between 1954 and 1962, and Angola and Mozambique from Portugal in 1975.
fact: The Soviet Union, the communist state that grew out of the Russian Empire, broke up into 15 countries in 1991, including Ukraine, Kazakhstan, Georgia and the Baltic states.
fact: Britain handed Hong Kong back to China in 1997 and Portugal handed Macau back in 1999, among the last pieces of the old European empires in Asia.
exercise: Who ruled most of the world's land and people in 1914? | European empires, with the USA and Japan | The United Nations, from its headquarters | The African Union | The Ottoman Empire alone, from Istanbul
exercise: How much of the world's land did the British Empire cover at its largest? | About a quarter | About half of it | About a tenth of it | Almost all of it
exercise: Which four empires broke up after the First World War? | Austro-Hungarian, Russian, German and Ottoman | British, French, Spanish and Portuguese, all at once | Mughal, Ming, Safavid and Qing | Roman, Greek, Persian and Egyptian
exercise: Which country appeared or reappeared on the map after the First World War? | Poland | Argentina | Indonesia | Ethiopia
exercise: When was the Republic of Turkey founded? | 1923 | 1453 | 1947 | 1991
exercise: Who drew the borders of many Middle Eastern countries after 1918? | Britain and France | The Ottoman sultan | The United Nations in New York | The local village elders
exercise: When did most of Spanish America and Brazil become independent? | Between about 1810 and 1825 | Between 1945 and 1975 | In 1492, when Columbus arrived | In 1991
exercise: Who was Simón Bolívar? | A leader of independence in South America | A Spanish king who ruled Mexico | A Portuguese explorer of the Amazon river | A British governor of India
exercise: When did most colonies in Asia and Africa become independent? | Between about 1945 and 1975 | Between 1810 and 1825 | Before 1700, after the Thirty Years' War | After 2000
exercise: Why did so many countries become independent after 1945? | Long campaigns by colonised peoples and weakened empires after the war | The empires were all bought up by the USA and then set free one by one | Every colony won a lottery | The Pope ordered the empires to end
exercise: In which year did India win independence? | 1947 | 1922 | 1960 | 1997
exercise: What did Gandhi's Salt March protest against? | A British tax on salt | A ban on fishing in the sea | The price of tea in Britain | The building of a big dam
exercise: Which was the first sub-Saharan African colony to become independent? | Ghana | Algeria | Angola | South Sudan
exercise: Why is 1960 called the Year of Africa? | 17 African countries became independent that year | The African Union was founded in Addis Ababa that year | Africa's borders were first drawn that year | All African colonies became free that year
exercise: Which colony won independence from France after a war from 1954 to 1962? | Algeria | India | Brazil | Madagascar
exercise: How many countries came out of the Soviet Union in 1991? | 15 | 2 | 50 | 193
exercise: When did Hong Kong return to China? | 1997 | 1947 | 1999 | 1842
test: In EU5 the colonial map of the Americas looks rock solid in 1836. What is the truth? | Most of Spanish America and Brazil were already independent by then | The colonies stayed under Spain and Portugal until about 1990 | The Americas were empty until 1836 | Spain still held Brazil
test: My mate says the Soviet Union just turned into Russia, simple. Right? | No, it split into 15 countries, including Ukraine and Kazakhstan | Yes, it all became one Russia | No, it joined the European Union as one big member | Yes, and Ukraine was never a separate country
test: If the First World War had not broken the four empires, which country would most likely not have reappeared in 1918? | Poland | Argentina | Portugal | Thailand
test: Why did UN membership jump from 51 in 1945 to more than 140 by 1975? | Many colonies became independent countries and joined | The UN started letting big cities like London and Tokyo join | Countries split into pieces for fun | Every country joined twice
test: did every colony just get handed freedom nice n easy | No, some like Algeria, Angola and Mozambique had to fight long wars | Yes, every empire left politely and said sorry on the way out | Yes, and they paid the colonies too | No, but only in South America
test: A friend says Gandhi won India's freedom with a big army. What is wrong? | He led peaceful campaigns such as the Salt March | Nothing, Gandhi was a great general who beat the British | Gandhi was British | India won freedom in 1922
test: Your grandma says she remembers when Macau was Portuguese. Could she? | Yes, Portugal handed it back only in 1999 | No, Macau was never Portuguese at all | No, it was handed back to China in 1550 | Yes, but only in the 1800s
test: Which happened last? | The Soviet Union broke up | India won independence | The Year of Africa | The Ottoman Empire came to an end
test: Britain and France drew straight-line borders for Iraq, Syria and Jordan. What earlier lesson does this match? | Outsiders drawing borders on maps, as in Africa | Borders always follow rivers and mountain ridges | Borders drawn by local people | Borders that never change
test: wait, Turkey isn't the Ottoman Empire? | No, the empire ended in 1922 and the republic began in 1923 | Yes, it is the same empire under a new flag | No, Turkey was part of Greece | Yes, the sultan still rules it from his palace in Istanbul
test: Which empire around 1920 covered about a quarter of the world's land? | The British Empire | The Ottoman Empire | The Mughal Empire | The Spanish Empire
exam: Identify the empires which were dissolved in consequence of the First World War. | The Austro-Hungarian, Russian, German and Ottoman empires | The British, French, Dutch and Portuguese empires | The Mughal, Safavid and Qing empires | The Spanish and Venetian dominions in the Americas
exam: State the designation conferred upon the year 1960 in respect of the African continent, and the reason therefor. | The Year of Africa, seventeen states having attained independence therein | The Year of Partition, Africa having been divided at Berlin | The Year of Union, the African Union having been constituted | The Year of Empire, Britain having annexed Africa
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the dissolution of the Union of Soviet Socialist Republics in 1991? | It was succeeded by fifteen independent states | It was absorbed in its entirety by the European Union | It was succeeded by a single state alone | It was partitioned between India and Pakistan
exam: Ascertain the years in which Hong Kong and Macau were respectively restored to China. | 1997 and 1999 | 1947 and 1960 | 1922 and 1923 | 1810 and 1825
